    "https://www.ccis.edu/careers/jobs?gnk=job&gni=8a7883a8a0abfc6b01a0c9bde5c96d16&lang=en": {
      "title": "Adjunct Instructor – U.S. History",
      "description": "Adjunct Instructor – U.S. History.  Adjunct Instructor – U.S. History School: Arts & Sciences Location: Columbia, MO Type: Academic Pay: $2500 per 16 week course ($833.33 per credit hour) for courses with 7-20 enrollments. Pay rate may vary for low- or high-enrolled courses. Applicants must be legally authorized to work in the United States; visa sponsorship is not available for this position. Job Summary: The Department of Humanities at Columbia College seeks an adjunct (part-time) instructor to teach HIST 121: American History to 1877 during the Spring 2027 semester. This is a U.S. history class covering 1877 to the present day. It is an in-seat, synchronous, general education class taught on our home campus in Columbia, Missouri. Classes start on 11 January, and this class meets Mondays, Wednesdays, and Fridays, 10:10 to 11:05 a.m. Qualifications: Minimum qualifications include a Master's Degree in History, with graduate coursework in U.S. history content. College teaching experience preferred. Graduate transcripts must be submitted with application materials. In addition, please submit an updated CV, and an application letter which discusses teaching experience and degree information. Physical Requirements: The physical requirements described here are representative of those that must be met by an employee to successfully perform the essential functions of this job. Accommodations may be made to enable individuals with disabilities to perform the essential functions. Individuals seeking accommodation should contact the Human Resources Department without delay. While performing the duties of this job, the employee is regularly required to sit for long periods of time, talk, and hear. The employee is required to use hands and fingers to handle or feel; reach with hands and arms; climb or balance; and stoop, kneel, or crawl. Employee must be able to type on a computer keyboard and use a computer monitor on a regular basis. Work Conditions: The work environment conditions described here are representative of those an employee encounters while performing the essential functions of this job. While performing the duties of this job, the employee is rarely exposed to outside weather conditions; however, possible exposure to hazards and physical risks to personal safety may occur while operating certain equipment. The noise level in the work environment is usually mild. This position is designated by the institution as a “Campus Security Authority” (CSA). As a CSA, you are required to report any information you receive about crimes occurring on or near campus to the Director of Campus Safety. This job description is not meant to be all-inclusive of every duty and responsibility required by the employee in the position. Eligibility for employee benefits and perks is determined by employment status. For more information please see https://www.ccis.edu/careers. The offer of employment and assignment to duties is contingent upon a satisfactory criminal background check. The information may include, but is not limited to, academic, residential, achievement, performance, attendance, disciplinary, employment history, credit history, driving history, and criminal history of public record. Columbia College is committed to creating an inclusive employee experience for all regardless of race, color, national origin, gender, religion, sexual orientation, age or disability. The college maintains a safe place for open discourse and overall, fosters a sense of community that welcomes everyone. In compliance with the Higher Education Opportunity Act (HEOA) and the Jeanne Clery Campus Safety Act (Clery Act), the Department of Campus Safety for Columbia College has provided the Annual Security and Fire Safety Report and crime statistics for the main campus and venues nationwide. Columbia College is required to distribute this information to all current and prospective employees and students. Please follow the link below for the full report or contact Human Resources at 573-875-7495 for a printed copy. http://www.ccis.edu/offices/campussafety/reportsandstats.aspx Apply for this Position Apply with Indeed Go back to the job list powered by"
    },
    "https://www.ccis.edu/careers/jobs?gnk=job&gni=8a78859ea064211b01a0a55e93bd1f3e&lang=en": {
      "title": "MSW Program Director",
      "description": "MSW Program Director.  MSW Program Director Department: Social Work, Human Services, & Criminal Justice Location: Columbia, MO or Remote possibility Type: Faculty, Full time, 12-month position Proposed Start Date: January 2027 Pay: $82,667-$90,000 Applicants must be legally authorized to work in the United States; visa sponsorship is not available for this position. Job Summary: Columbia College (MO) is seeking a collaborative, innovative, and student-centered academic leader to serve as Director of the Master of Social Work (MSW) Program. This is a full-time, 12-month, Assistant or Associate Professor, tenure-track position anticipated to begin January 2027. The MSW Program Director provides academic, administrative, and strategic leadership for Columbia College’s online MSW program. The MSW Program Director is responsible for the overall quality, integrity, effectiveness, and continuous improvement of the MSW program and ensures that the program operates in alignment with the mission and strategic priorities of Columbia College, the School of Public Service, and the Educational Policy and Accreditation Standards (EPAS) of the Council on Social Work Education (CSWE). Columbia College’s MSW Program currently holds candidacy status with the Council on Social Work Education (CSWE), with full accreditation anticipated in October 2027. The MSW Program Director will provide support to the Dean of the School of Public Service in preparation of accreditation documents for initial accreditation. The MSW Program Director works collaboratively with the Department Chair, social work faculty, Practicum Director, academic support units, enrollment and admissions personnel, and other institutional partners to promote academic excellence, student success, faculty development, program growth, and professional preparation. This position includes teaching responsibilities primarily within the MSW program; however, there may be additional opportunities to teach in related disciplines. It also requires service and professional development expectations. Essential Functions: Provide day-to-day academic and administrative leadership for the MSW program in collaboration with the Department Chair and social work faculty. Establish program priorities, communicate program updates, facilitate collaborative decision-making, and recommend policies and procedures that support effective program operations. Represent the MSW program within the School, College, and appropriate institutional committees and initiatives. Lead ongoing compliance with CSWE Educational Policy and Accreditation Standards in collaboration with the Department Chair, Practicum Director, faculty, and institutional partners. Coordinate and maintain accreditation documentation, reports, assessment evidence, substantive change materials, and other required submissions. Monitor accreditation requirements, prepare for reviews and site visits, and ensure the program remains current with CSWE standards and expectations. Lead the ongoing development, review, implementation, and revision of the MSW curriculum to ensure rigor, consistency, currency, and alignment with program goals and CSWE competencies. Lead implementation of the MSW assessment plan, including collection, analysis, interpretation, and reporting of student learning and program outcome data. Collaborate with faculty to evaluate student achievement of CSWE competencies and use assessment findings to guide continuous program improvement. Recommend faculty teaching assignments based on expertise, program needs, accreditation requirements, and workload considerations. Support orientation, communication, mentoring, and professional development of full-time and part-time faculty. Collaborate with admissions, enrollment, marketing, advising, and faculty to support recruitment, enrollment, orientation, retention, and student success. Serve as a resource for students and promote engagement, belonging, professional identity development, licensure preparation, leadership, and connection to the social work profession. Collaborate with the MSW Practicum Director to integrate classroom and practicum education. Support practicum policies, curriculum connections, and development of community partnerships that expand high-quality practicum opportunities. Identify opportunities for sustainable growth and innovation based on student needs, workforce trends, community needs, and developments within the social work profession. Teach 15 credit hours per academic year, with teaching responsibilities adjusted to reflect the administrative responsibilities of the Program Director position. Maintain professional competence and engage in scholarly, professional, practice, and service activities consistent with faculty expectations.Model ethical and professional conduct consistent with the NASW Code of Ethics and the values of the social work profession. Qualifications: The successful candidate will hold a doctoral degree in social work (DSW or Ph.D.), a Master’s degree in Social Work (MSW) from a CSWE-accredited program and two or more years of post-MSW practice experience. Preferred Qualifications The ideal candidate will hold a master’s-level social work license and have experience in higher education and academic leadership. Preferred qualifications include knowledge of DSW programs and accreditation standards, a record of effective, student-centered teaching, experience with innovative, online instructional methods, and experience developing and teaching asynchronous courses. To Apply: Interested applicants may apply online at www.ccis.edu/jobs. Date of first consideration is September 30, 2026 and review of applications will continue until the position is filled. To be considered for this position, please submit the following: Completed Columbia College application Cover letter Curriculum vitae Names and contact information for three references from professional colleagues Leadership philosophy Copies of transcripts (official transcripts are required before employment) Physical Requirements: The physical requirements described here are representative of those that must be met by an employee to successfully perform the essential functions of this job. Accommodations may be made to enable individuals with disabilities to perform the essential functions. Individuals seeking accommodation should contact the Human Resources Department without delay. While performing the duties of this job, the employee is regularly required to sit for long periods of time, talk, and hear. The employee is required to use hands and fingers to handle or feel; reach with hands and arms; climb or balance; and stoop, kneel, or crawl. Employee must be able to type on a computer keyboard and use a computer monitor on a regular basis. Work Conditions: The work environment conditions described here are representative of those an employee encounters while performing the essential functions of this job. While performing the duties of this job, the employee is rarely exposed to outside weather conditions; however, possible exposure to hazards and physical risks to personal safety may occur while operating certain equipment. The noise level in the work environment is usually mild. This position is designated by the institution as a “Campus Security Authority” (CSA). As a CSA, you are required to report any information you receive about crimes occurring on or near campus to the Director of Campus Safety. This job description is not meant to be all-inclusive of every duty and responsibility required by the employee in the position. Eligibility for employee benefits and perks is determined by employment status. For more information please see https://www.ccis.edu/careers. The offer of employment and assignment to duties is contingent upon a satisfactory criminal background check. The information may include, but is not limited to, academic, residential, achievement, performance, attendance, disciplinary, employment history, credit history, driving history, and criminal history of public record. Columbia College is committed to creating an inclusive employee experience for all regardless of race, color, national origin, gender, religion, sexual orientation, age or disability. The college maintains a safe place for open discourse and overall, fosters a sense of community that welcomes everyone. In compliance with the Higher Education Opportunity Act (HEOA) and the Jeanne Clery Campus Safety Act (Clery Act), the Department of Campus Safety for Columbia College has provided the Annual Security and Fire Safety Report and crime statistics for the main campus and venues nationwide. Columbia College is required to distribute this information to all current and prospective employees and students. Please follow the link below for the full report or contact Human Resources at 573-875-7495 for a printed copy. http://www.ccis.edu/offices/campussafety/reportsandstats.aspx Apply for this Position Apply with Indeed Go back to the job list powered by"
    },
    "https://www.ccis.edu/careers/jobs?gnk=job&gni=8a78859f91e3135a0192007cc5757314&lang=en": {
      "title": "Center for Social Media and Alumni Communications Department: Academic Affairs",
      "description": "Center for Social Media and Alumni Communications Department: Academic Affairs.  Student Position: Center for Social Media and Alumni Communications Department: Academic Affairs Location: Columbia, MO Type: Student Employment, Part-time Pay: $15 (Pay range may vary based on geographic location) Applicants must be legally authorized to work in the United States; visa sponsorship is not available for this position. Job Summary: CSMAC is seeking part-time student employee to assist with a range of marketing and communications initiatives that support the strategic plan of the Robert W. Plaster School of Business (RWPSOB). Primary tasks include assisting with design and execution of social media campaigns, drafting content calendars for the RWPSOB social media pages, and updating an alumni database with up-to-date information. Minimum qualifications include considerable progress towards a bachelor’s degree and excellent organizational and communication skills. Prior experience with graphic design or social media is preferred but not required. Applicants must be current Columbia College students. Applications are accepted and reviewed on a rolling basis. Essential Functions: Assist with the design and execution of social media campaigns Draft and revise content calendars for the RWPSOB Facebook, LinkedIn, and Instagram Track social media engagement to identify successful content and campaigns Update and maintain accurate records of RWPSOB alumni Capture and edit photos and videos as needed for campaigns Participate in CSMAC meetings Attend campus events Skills: · Ability to fluently read, write and understand the English language. · Proficiency for using a variety of computer and technology-related software and equipment, including Microsoft Office products (Excel) and social media platforms. · Ability to work independently or in a team environment, with or without direct supervision. · Excellent communication, interpersonal, customer service and organizational skills with all constituents. Education: · Significant progress towards a bachelor’s degree Physical Requirements: The physical demands described here are representative of those that must be met by an employee to successfully perform the essential functions of this job. Accommodations may be made to enable individuals with disabilities to perform the essential functions. While performing the duties of this job, the employee is regularly required to sit for long periods of time and talk or hear. The employee is regularly required to use hands and fingers to handle or feel; reach with hands and arms; climb or balance; and stoop, kneel, or crawl. The employee must lift, carry and move a minimum of pounds on an occasional basis. Must be able to type on a computer keyboard and use a computer monitor on a frequent and regular basis. Work Conditions: The work environment characteristics described here are representative of those an employee encounters while performing the essential functions of this job. While performing the duties of this job, the employee is rarely exposed to outside weather conditions; however, possible exposure to hazards and physical risks to personal safety may occur while operating certain equipment. The noise level in the work environment is usually mild. This job description is not meant to be all-inclusive of every duty and responsibility required by the employee in the position. The offer of employment and assignment to duties is contingent upon a satisfactory criminal background check. The information may include, but is not limited to, academic, residential, achievement, performance, attendance, disciplinary, employment history, credit history, driving history, and criminal history of public record. Review of applications will begin immediately and continue until the position is filled. Columbia College is an equal opportunity employer. “Columbia College is committed to creating an inclusive employee experience for all regardless of race, color, national origin, gender, religion, sexual orientation, age or disability. The college maintains a safe place for open discourse; supports activities that promote diversity and inclusion; and overall, fosters a sense of community that welcomes everyone.” In compliance with the Higher Education Opportunity Act (HEOA) and the Jeanne Clery Campus Safety Act (Clery Act), the Department of Campus Safety for Columbia College has provided the Annual Security and Fire Safety Report and crime statistics for the main campus and venues nationwide. Columbia College is required to distribute this information to all current and prospective employees and students. Please follow the link below for the full report or contact Human Resources at 573-875-7495 for a printed copy. http://www.ccis.edu/offices/campussafety/reportsandstats.aspx Apply for this Position Apply with Indeed Go back to the job list powered by"
    },
    "https://www.ccis.edu/careers/jobs?gnk=job&gni=8a78879e9fd3ba5c019ffa0c26783c36&lang=en": {
      "title": "Assistant Softball Coach",
      "description": "Assistant Softball Coach.  Assistant Softball Coach Department: Athletics Location: Columbia, MO Type: Staff, Full-time (10-Month) Pay: $30,000 per year Applicants must be legally authorized to work in the United States; visa sponsorship is not available for this position. Job Summary: The Assistant Softball Coach will provide overall guidance to the softball program. Assist the Head Coach with the day-to-day operations of the program including, but not limited to, the following tasks. Essential Functions: Identification of potential student-athletes, recruitment of qualified student-athletes, and maintenance of recruitment files. Strategic management of roster size in alignment with program expectations and departmental enrollment goals. Oversight and allocation of athletic scholarships/financial aid within established budget parameters. Demonstrate an understanding of net tuition revenue (NTR) and its impact on recruiting, scholarship distribution, and roster composition. Work collaboratively with Admissions and Enrollment Management to support recruiting, yield, and retention strategies. Organization and coordination of daily practice plans. Game day preparations, including court set-up and break down, uniform and equipment preparation, game-plan preparation, warm-up activities, and game evaluation. Inventory management of uniforms and equipment and input into purchasing decisions on new equipment. Scheduling and travel plans, including the organization of lodging and meals on road trips, and timely completion of expense reports. Monitoring and supervision of student-athletes, to include athletic activities, academic progress, eligibility concerns and disciplinary issues. Coordination of student-athlete community service projects in conjunction with the NAIA Champions of Character program. Participation in program and athletic department fund-raising efforts. Department assistance with game day management throughout the year. Work effectively with students, student-athletes, administrators, faculty, staff, parents, alumni, officials, and coaching peers. Minimum Qualifications: Prior coaching experience Preferred Qualifications: Master’s Degree from an accredited Institution At least five years NAIA/NCAA head coaching experience Physical Requirements: The physical requirements described here are representative of those that must be met by an employee to successfully perform the essential functions of this job. Accommodations may be made to enable individuals with disabilities to perform the essential functions. Individuals seeking accommodation should contact the Human Resources Department without delay. While performing the duties of this job, the employee is regularly required to sit for long periods of time, talk, and hear. The employee is required to use hands and fingers to handle or feel; reach with hands and arms; climb or balance; and stoop, kneel, or crawl. Employee must be able to type on a computer keyboard and use a computer monitor on a regular basis. Work Conditions: The work environment conditions described here are representative of those an employee encounters while performing the essential functions of this job. While performing the duties of this job, the employee is rarely exposed to outside weather conditions; however, possible exposure to hazards and physical risks to personal safety may occur while operating certain equipment. The noise level in the work environment is usually mild. This position is designated by the institution as a “Campus Security Authority” (CSA). As a CSA, you are required to report any information you receive about crimes occurring on or near campus to the Director of Campus Safety. This job description is not meant to be all-inclusive of every duty and responsibility required by the employee in the position. Eligibility for employee benefits and perks is determined by employment status. For more information please see https://www.ccis.edu/careers. The offer of employment and assignment to duties is contingent upon a satisfactory criminal background check. The information may include, but is not limited to, academic, residential, achievement, performance, attendance, disciplinary, employment history, credit history, driving history, and criminal history of public record. Columbia College is committed to creating an inclusive employee experience for all regardless of race, color, national origin, gender, religion, sexual orientation, age or disability. The college maintains a safe place for open discourse and overall, fosters a sense of community that welcomes everyone. In compliance with the Higher Education Opportunity Act (HEOA) and the Jeanne Clery Campus Safety Act (Clery Act), the Department of Campus Safety for Columbia College has provided the Annual Security and Fire Safety Report and crime statistics for the main campus and venues nationwide. Columbia College is required to distribute this information to all current and prospective employees and students. Please follow the link below for the full report or contact Human Resources at 573-875-7495 for a printed copy. http://www.ccis.edu/offices/campussafety/reportsandstats.aspx Apply for this Position Apply with Indeed Go back to the job list powered by"
